## Receipts audit (`03-receipts.md`, opened only after the above was written)

Checked as a claim, not accepted as a report. Most of it holds against the page:

- **Verified true.** The four table rows the receipts say were added in round 1 are
  present and worded as described (rows 51, 54, 55, 60). The "The Vietnamese Magazine,
  trong bài tháng 12/2025, ghi nhận…" attribution is on the page (line 29). The round-2
  edits are on the page verbatim: the decree sentence at line 39 reads exactly as the
  receipts quote it; "các nghĩa vụ đã có hiệu lực" replaced a future framing; the new
  decree-delegation row is table row 64; the VCCI clause reads "không tìm thấy" (line
  25). The table does carry 20 rows, all graded B. Every source in it is one
  `01-research.md` already held — the receipts' claim that no new source was invented is
  true. The "7–10 years" compression to "nhiều năm" (line 35) is real and honestly
  described.
- **Verified true and unusually candid.** The receipts' word-count method (exhaustive
  `\S+` token dump, then a line count of the dump) is reproducible and its stated
  limitation — that this is a *token* count running a few percent above a semantic
  Vietnamese word count — is correct and volunteered. My independent reconstruction of
  their method lands at ~1,309 against their 1,305. They also correct two of their own
  earlier overstatements rather than repeating them (prior "~1,290 words" → actual
  1,309 pre-fix, over the band; and withdrawing an "every time they appear" claim about
  instrument numbers). That is the right behaviour for a receipts file.
- **One receipts claim does not hold against the page — and it is the same one as my
  first finding.** Receipts L197-201 state: *"Checked every other instance of 'chưa ban
  hành,' 'không có … nào,' and 'bắt đầu áp dụng' — neither overclaim was repeated
  anywhere else in the body or in the confidence table."* The sweep missed **"chưa
  có … nào"**, which is the same overclaim in the same class, and it survives in the
  very sentence round 2 rewrote: line 39's "chưa có tuyên bố công khai nào". The
  receipts' assurance that the class of defect was swept out is therefore not supported
  by the artifact. This is an audit finding in its own right (a claimed step whose
  result is not visible on the page) and it independently corroborates Finding 1 as a
  genuine survivor rather than a nitpick.
- **Silent on the quotation-mark issue.** The receipts nowhere address whether quoted
  Vietnamese strings were checked against the fact store's English wording. Neutral —
  not a false claim, but the second finding was not looked for.

---

## Minors (not blocking)

None of these is false, unsupported or self-contradictory. Each is something I would
have done differently.

- Line 5 drops "có mặt" from "434/443 đại biểu **có mặt** tán thành". The reader must
  infer that 443 is delegates present, not the total, to reconcile it with 91,75%. One
  word restores it.
- The section head "Ba phía tranh cãi" is followed by four enumerated paragraphs ("Thứ
  nhất… Thứ hai… Thứ ba… **Cuối cùng**"). The fourth is a corrective note about the
  "bundled package" framing, not a fourth side; the signposting invites the reader to
  count it as one.
- Line 23 promises "mỗi luồng ý kiến dưới đây gắn rõ **chủ thể** và thời điểm", and
  strand two (line 27) then has no subject at all. The piece says so immediately and
  honestly, so nothing false ships — but the promise over-reaches its own section by
  one strand.
- "Hồ sơ nghiên cứu" (the internal research file) is named twice in body prose (lines
  27, 39) in a piece written for a general audience. Honest, but it exposes pipeline
  furniture to a reader who has no idea what it is; "theo các nguồn tra được" would
  carry the same epistemic weight.
- Table row 55 is the weakest claim in the piece: graded B with a source column that
  names no source. It is hedged in the prose ("được mô tả là") and openly flagged in the
  table, which is why it is not a finding — but under Axiom 6 read strictly, a claim
  with no domain behind it is closer to C, and the cleanest treatment would have been to
  cut it. It carries no weight in the argument. `01-research.md` L77-79 also attaches a
  caveat to that bullet (the sanctions half "may partly describe the adjacent Personal
  Data Protection Law"); the deliverable drops the sanctions half entirely, which
  neutralises the caveat, but does not carry it.
- Two body claims have no dedicated table row: the law-firm "rủi ro tuân thủ" analyses
  (line 25; sourced in research L91-94) and the absence of a named critic of
  centralisation (line 27). Both are sourced or self-describing in the prose, so the
  table is incomplete rather than inconsistent.
- The piece never anchors itself in time for the reader. Shipping on 2026-09-01, two
  months after commencement, the only signal that the law is already operating is "các
  nghĩa vụ đã có hiệu lực" in the final clause; "Điều đáng theo dõi **từ** 1/7/2026"
  reads as prospective. One clause ("đã có hiệu lực được hai tháng") would fix the frame.
- `01-research.md` L20-22 records that the Ministry of Public Security ran a public
  comment period 28/6–13/7/2025. The piece omits it while carrying the "kín tiếng" /
  fast-track characterisation. Omitting a fact that cuts against the piece's own framing
  is a fairness cost, not an accuracy one.
- Line 5's "cả hai bị bãi bỏ khi luật mới có hiệu lực" is temporally unmarked Vietnamese
  and reads slightly prospective for a piece published after commencement.

---

## Verdict: FIX-IT

Two items are checkably unsupported or self-contradictory as shipped. Both are honesty
defects rather than fact errors — which is the axis that matters most on this topic:

1. **Line 39** — "các hiệp hội doanh nghiệp nước ngoài — **chưa có tuyên bố công khai
   nào**" asserts an absence. `01-research.md` L91-95/L188-189 supports only "no citable
   statement found … a gap, not a confirmed absence", and the same deliverable states
   the supported version at line 25 and in table row 57. Rewrite as a statement about
   the search, not about the world. `03-receipts.md` L197-201 claims this class of
   overclaim was swept for and found nowhere else; the sweep pattern omitted "chưa
   có … nào" and this instance survived in the sentence that round was rewriting.
2. **Quotation marks around back-translated strings** (lines 17, 25, 29, 35) — the fact
   store holds these only in English. Most consequential: the official-rationale quote
   at line 35, printed as the Vietnamese government's own Vietnamese words when
   `01-research.md` holds only an English snippet rendering, and printed with "yêu cầu"
   and "quốc gia" absent. Either mark them as translations from English summaries, or
   drop the quotation marks.

Everything else on this page is sound. All six traps the axis was set to catch are
handled — several better than merely avoided, with the piece naming the misattribution
and refusing it in the reader's hearing. Every figure, date, instrument number and
source attribution traces to `01-research.md` with the same value and the same source;
no source is invented; the B-ceiling is declared in plain body prose; the plan was
followed and the word budget met on two independent counts. This is a FIX-IT for two
sentences' worth of over-claim, not a judgement on the work as a whole.

**Note for the record:** this run is frozen; no repair round will follow. The two items
above therefore stand as the closing description of the artifact's state, not as a work
order. A reader relying on this piece should read the AmCham/EuroCham clause in the
final sentence as "not found" rather than "does not exist", and treat every string in
quotation marks as a translation rather than as verbatim wording. Nothing in the piece
is factually wrong on the record available. The truth-ceiling of the entire run is **B**,
because egress was blocked and no source was opened — by the maker or by me.
